{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481690,"nonce":"rGQKcg2KnnWFAAkjbWdKgkpT/mM=","title":"**What is wrong.** `internal/drill` is a five-package bounded context (`internal/drill` itself plus `api`, `domain`, `repository`, `service`) that does not appear in the CONTEXT.md §4 module table or in its dependency-direction diagram. It therefore has no declared edges — and it needs none, because it reaches its neighbours by writing their table names into SQL rather than by importing their packages. `grep -in drill CONTEXT.md` returns nothing.","message":"**The demonstration.** `internal/drill/repository/artifacts.go` reads 12 tables owned by six other modules: `ingest_batches` and `ingest_rejections` (ingestion), `alerts` and `alert_occurrences` (alerts), `alert_groups` and `alert_group_members` (grouping), `notifications`, `notification_deliveries` and `notification_policies` (notification), `channels` and `channel_threads` (channels), `rule_snapshots` (rules). `internal/drill/repository/dispose.go` deletes from four more of them: `alert_events` (`:71`), `channel_threads` (`:86`), `alert_groups` (`:94`) and `alerts` (`:109`).\n\nMeanwhile the production code in `internal/drill/**` imports exactly two non-`platform` internal packages, and both are its own: `drill/domain` and `drill/service`. The one cross-module Go reference in the whole context is `internal/ingestion/decode` in `internal/drill/domain/payload_test.go:12` — a test file. So every import-based guard is blind here by construction. `.golangci.yml` carries three drill entries (`:161`, `:207`, `:253`) and all three are intra-module layering rules — `api` must not import `repository`, `repository` must not import `api`, `domain` stays pure. There is no rule about drill and another module because there is no import for one to constrain. `grep -c drill` returns 0 in both `tools/lintreach/baseline.txt` and `tools/lintvocab/baseline.txt`.\n\nCONTEXT.md §5 rule 4: *\"Cross-domain calls are **service → service**, through an interface declared by the **consumer**. Never repository → repository.\"* A repository issuing raw SQL against another module's tables is the coupling that rule forbids, with the compiler removed from the enforcement path. The dependency diagram in §4 is introduced as *\"Dependency direction (no cycles, enforced by `depguard` + an arch test)\"*, and drill is outside both mechanisms.\n\nThe deletes themselves are argued carefully and this ticket does not dispute them: `dispose.go:21-49` sets out the ADR 0024 carve-out (a drill records no upstream event, so deleting it destroys no history), every statement is scoped by an id from the drill's own manifest, and `AND synthetic` is a belt-and-braces predicate on the two riskiest ones. The defect is that the reasoning lives in a comment on a file nothing in the build system knows is special. `Dispose` is the only function in oto that deletes a signal row, and it does so through four table names that no linter, no import graph and no architecture document connects to their owners.\n\n**Why it matters.** Every guard oto has against a module reaching where it should not — depguard's per-module blocks, the arch test, the §4 arrows — operates on the Go import graph. drill's coupling is entirely below that layer, so the module can acquire a dependency on any table in the schema without a single review signal firing. The specific failure this makes possible is a schema change in `notification` or `grouping` that no `drill` maintainer sees coming, and whose breakage surfaces as a runtime SQL error on the drill path. The second is subtler and worse: a future edit to `dispose.go` that widens a predicate, in a file whose danger is documented only in prose, deleting a real customer's alert history.\n\n**Where.** `internal/drill/repository/artifacts.go` (the 12 reads), `internal/drill/repository/dispose.go:50-122` (the four deletes), `internal/drill/doc.go`, `CONTEXT.md` §4 (the missing row and the missing arrows), `.golangci.yml:161,207,253` (the three rules that exist, none cross-module).\n\n**Scope.** large\n\n**Done when.** `drill` has a row in the CONTEXT.md §4 module table and edges in the dependency diagram that match what it actually touches; the artifact reads and the disposal deletes go through ports drill declares and `internal/app/container.go` satisfies from the owning modules' services, so that depguard and the arch test can see the coupling — or, if the direct-SQL design is deliberately kept, a linter enforces SQL table ownership so that a new table name in `internal/drill/**` fails CI the way a new import would.","files":null}]}